"O-VAD/README.md at main · o-vad/O-VAD · GitHub (https://github.com/o-vad/O-VAD/blob/main/README.md)\nciteturn28search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; O-VAD is a training-free agentic framework. ... Instead of predicting a binary normal/abnormal label, it reasons about object state evolution and emits a structured report naming the abnormal object, the frames where the anomaly occurs, its type and severity, and a causal explanation.\n\n# O-VAD: Industrial Video Anomaly Detection through Object-Centric Tracking and Reasoning\n\n### Project Page | Paper | Code\n\nOfficial implementation of the ECCV 2026 paper \"O-VAD: Industrial Video Anomaly Detection through Object-Centric Tracking and Reasoning\".\n\nO-VAD is a training-free agentic framework. It requires no fine-tuning, no domain-knowledge injection, and no predefined anomaly taxonomy. Instead of predicting a binary normal/abnormal label, it reasons about object state evolution and emits a structured report naming the abnormal object, the frames where the anomaly occurs, its type and severity, and a causal explanation.\n\n(a) Object Grounding — a VLM lists the distinct objects in the scene and describes their material and initial state; SAM turns those phrases into a first-frame mask prompt. (b) Object-Centric State Tracking — CropFormer + SAM build spatiotemporal partitions, held together by semantic and proximity constraints, and a VLM labels the state changes and interactions between them. (c) State-Aware Anomaly Reasoning — a chain of thought runs from process understanding through observation, expectation, comparison and causation to classification and severity, with a post-verification pass over the candidate anomaly frames, producing a report at the video, frame and object level.\n\n> O-VAD's Stage 2 is built on TubeletGraph (NeurIPS 2025). This repository carries only the parts of it that O-VAD executes; for VOST/VSCOS/M³-VOS tracking evaluation, see the upstream repository. This file is the complete O-VAD guide.\n\n## 1. Pipeline Overview\n\nO-VAD runs ground → track → reason. Each stage writes to disk, so a crashed run resumes from the last completed stage rather than restarting.\n\nStage  | What it does  | Models  | Code\n--- | --- | --- | ---\n1. Object Grounding  | A VLM enumerates the objects in the scene as a structured inventory; SAM3 converts each text prompt into a first-frame mask. A second VLM call produces a temporal caption and a recommended FPS (2–10) for Stage 2.  | VLM + SAM3  | annotate/vlm_mask_grounded.py\n2. Object-Centric State Tracking  | Builds spatiotemporal tubelets from the Stage 1 masks, keeping identity through deformation, splitting, and occlusion via spatial proximity + CLIP semantic consistency. A VLM labels state-change events on the resulting graph.  | CropFormer + SAM2 + FC-CLIP + VLM  | TubeletGraph/run.py, TubeletGraph/vlm/prompt_vlm.py\n3. State-Aware Reasoning  | Six-step chain-of-thought over the state graph (observation → expectation → comparison → causation → classification → severity), separating expected process actions from failure outcomes, with confidence-gated visual verification.  | VLM  | TubeletGraph/vlm/prompt_vad.py\n\nThe three stages are orchestrated by pipeline.py, which handles video decoding, VQA-file filtering, dynamic FPS, scratch cleanup, and report writing. One driver serves all benchmarks: everything dataset-specific (on-disk layout, storage-path anchoring, sample discovery, grounding prompts) lives in a `DatasetAdapter` subclass selected by `--dataset`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nO-VAD: Industrial Video Anomaly Detection through Object-Centric Tracking and Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.18142)\nciteturn28academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Title: O-VAD: Industrial Video Anomaly Detection through Object-Centric Tracking and Reasoning ... To tackle the complexity of such interaction-intensive detection, we introduce a training-free agentic framework for anomaly detection free of domain-specific knowledge, emphasizing object state evolution like humans inspectors.\nTitle: O-VAD: Industrial Video Anomaly Detection through Object-Centric Tracking and Reasoning\nAuthors: Mei Yuan, Qi Long, Qifeng Wu, Zhenyang Li, Yizhou Zhao, Lei Wang, Yang Liu, Min Xu\nDate: Mon Jul 20 16:36:00 2026\n\nIndustrial Video Anomaly Detection (IVAD) aims to identify anomalous objects and events in an industrial process, which is crucial for modern manufacturing and quality control systems. Existing VLM-based anomaly reasoning methods are capable of detecting open-ended anomalies in general domains. However, their performance declines in industrial settings characterized by intricate object transformations, strict physics, and procedural constraints. To tackle the complexity of such interaction-intensive detection, we introduce a training-free agentic framework for anomaly detection free of domain-specific knowledge, emphasizing object state evolution like humans inspectors. It is designed to track spatial-temporal dynamics and underlying transformations of detected objects over time, and then reason over the object-wise temporal state trajectories to identify abnormal objects in grounded frames. Our method overcomes limitations of prior approaches that rely on retraining on normal clips or injecting domain knowledge as context for test-time inference. Extensive experiments on three IVAD datasets demonstrate that our method outperforms frontier VLMs, agentic frameworks, and traditional VAD methods fine-tuned on the respective datasets, while providing interpretable reports over anomaly processes and types.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nO-VAD tracks object states to spot industrial… · AGI Hunt (https://agihunt.info/en/papers/2607.18142)\nciteturn28search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: last month; Training-free O-VAD tracks object states across frames, then reasons over the trajectories to flag anomalies, beating GPT-5 and fine-tuned VAD on AUROC across three datasets. ... Each object k ends up with a trajectory Sk = {(mask m, state description phi)}, where the mask pins location and the natural-language description carries physical state.\n# O-VAD tracks object states to spot industrial defects, no retraining, beats fine-tuned VAD & GPT-5\n\nO-VAD: Industrial Video Anomaly Detection through Object-Centric Tracking and Reasoning\n\nMei Yuan, Qi Long, Qifeng Wu, Zhenyang Li, Yizhou Zhao, Lei Wang, Yang Liu, Min Xu\n\nECCV 2026\n\ncs.CV, cs.AI, cs.CL, cs.MA\n\n2026-07-21\n\nTraining-free O-VAD tracks object states across frames, then reasons over the trajectories to flag anomalies, beating GPT-5 and fine-tuned VAD on AUROC across three datasets.\n\n## What problem this solves\n\nIndustrial Video Anomaly Detection (IVAD) is a real production need: a chipped gear, an undertightened screw, or a leaking toothpaste tube all have to be caught at the quality-control step. Two families of methods exist, and each has a clear weakness.\n\nTraditional anomaly detectors follow a \"one class, one model\" recipe. Trained on abundant normal samples to reconstruct or embed frames, they only emit a scalar anomaly score, never say what went wrong, and must be retrained from scratch for every new product line. \nStage two tracks state, and this is the heart of the method. CropFormer does spatiotemporal segmentation, then SAM2 links the same object across frames into a tubelet. When a track breaks, it is patched back together using spatial proximity and semantic consistency. At every moment an object's state transitions, the VLM is queried for an open-ended description: when, what kind of change, why, how severe. Each object k ends up with a trajectory Sk = {(mask m, state description phi)}, where the mask pins location and the natural-language description carries physical state.\n\nStage three reasons. Each object's trajectory goes into a six-step chain of thought: process understanding, observation, expectation, comparison, causation, then classification and severity. It is asking what the normal procedure should look like, what is actually seen, where they diverge, why, and what kind of anomaly it is. A visual verification pass with multiplicative confidence gating filters hallucinated calls at the end.\n\nWhether object k in frame t is anomalous is decided by the VLM reasoning over the chain-of-thought prompt, that object's state trajectory, and the process context. That is the whole method in one sentence.\n\nThe design choice that matters is the state-trajectory representation. It forces attention onto each object's own evolution instead of diluting it with background, and the ablation shows the result lives or dies on this.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://o-vad.github.io/)\nciteturn28search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Given an industrial video, O‑VAD produces a structured anomaly report — type, severity, affected object, temporal localization, and a natural‑language causal explanation — without any domain‑specific knowledge, predefined taxonomy, or training data. ... Image: O-VAD framework: (a) Object Grounding via VLM inventory and SAM masks, (b) Object-Centric State Tracking with spatiotemporal partitions and state-change detection, (c) State-Aware Anomaly Reasoning producing the anomaly report\n## A ground → track → reason pipeline\n\nGiven an industrial video, O‑VAD produces a structured anomaly report — type, severity, affected object, temporal localization, and a natural‑language causal explanation — without any domain‑specific knowledge, predefined taxonomy, or training data. It runs in three agentic stages.\n\nImage: O-VAD framework: (a) Object Grounding via VLM inventory and SAM masks, (b) Object-Centric State Tracking with spatiotemporal partitions and state-change detection, (c) State-Aware Anomaly Reasoning producing the anomaly report\n\n1\n\n### Object Grounding\n\nSample frames spanning the video, query a VLM for a structured object inventory (name, material, initial state), then segment every object with SAM3 to produce initial masks and metadata.\n\nGPT‑5 SAM3\n\n2\n\n### Object‑Centric State Tracking\n\nBuild spatiotemporal tubelets with CropFormer entity segmentation and SAM2 propagation. Recover tracks lost through transformations using spatial‑proximity and CLIP semantic‑consistency priors, then query the VLM for open‑ended state‑change events — type, cause, severity, per object.\n\nCropFormer SAM2 CLIP\n\n3\n\n### State‑Aware Reasoning\n\nA cascaded six‑step chain‑of‑thought reasons over the accumulated state trajectories, separating expected process actions from failure outcomes.\n\n1 Process understanding\n\n2 Observation\n\n3 Expectation\n\n4 Comparison\n\n5 Causation\n\n6 Classification & severity\n\n+ confidence‑gated visual verification\n\n03 / Quantitative results\n\n## State of the art without any training\n\nWe foreground the metrics that matter for open-ended IVAD — detection AUROC (video and frame level) and anomaly-type description quality — and drop Acc/P/R/F1. O‑VAD posts the best video-level AUROC on all three datasets and the most faithful type descriptions, then leads or ties across most Phys-AD and IPAD categories.\n\nComprehensive results detection AUROC + anomaly‑type score · video & frame level\n\n | Phys-AD  | LiquidAD  | IPAD\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nMethod  | Vid AUROC ↑  | BERTScore ↑  | LLM‑judge ↑  | Vid AUROC ↑  | Frm AUROC ↑  | Vid AUROC ↑  | Frm AUROC ↑\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nTraditional VAD\nMNAD.p^{†}  | 0.495  | –  | –  | 0.453  | 0.487  | 0.522  | 0.430\nS3R^{†}  | 0.555  | –  | –  | 0.651  | 0.625  | 0.539  | 0.495\nDirect prompting\nQwen3‑VL^{∗}  | 0.513  | 0.798  | 0.372  | 0.489  | 0.499  | 0.498  | 0.497\nGPT‑5^{∗}  | 0.502  | 0.878  | 0.580  | 0.465  | 0.484  | 0.519  | 0.532\nAgentic workflow\nURF^{∗}  | 0.426  | –  | –  | 0.365  | 0.506  | 0.417  | 0.512\nVERA^{†}  | 0.456  | –  | –  | 0.534  | 0.500  | 0.519  | 0.490\nOurs\nO‑VAD^{∗}  | 0.584  | 0.803  | 0.595  | 0.692  | 0.512  | 0.565  | 0.518\n\nHigher is better; per row the best is boxed and the 2nd-best shaded; O‑VAD shown in accent. Phys-AD has no frame-level labels; LiquidAD and IPAD carry no free-form type score. † trained  ∗ training-free.\n\n04 / Qualitative results\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) OECD AI Capability Indicators Technical Report (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/405344635_OECD_AI_Capability_Indicators_Technical_Report)\nciteturn28search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; in long-lasting benchmarks to evalua te a robot system’s progression across multiple levels of robot ... To illustrate this multi-level benchmarking framework, consider an example benchmark for autonomous\n\nmedical triage (level 4-5).\n\n• Mohamed Bin Zayed International Robotics Challenge (ongoing): Biennial competition to foster\n\ninnovation in robotics with an emphasis on real-world deployments. Previous applications include\n\nmaritime security and firefighting (level 4-5).\n\n• World Robot Challenge (ongoing): Robotics competition accompanying the World Robot Summit.\n\nIn 2025, challenges include: future convenience store challenge, harsh environment challenge,\n\nmanufacturing robotics challenge (level 4-5).\n\n• CMU Vision-Language-Autonomy Challenge (ongoing): A challenge to develop real-world robots\n\nthat can take in natural language commands to navigate an environment (level 4-5).\n\nDesigning multi-level benchmarks for progressive capability assessment\n\nWhile numerous full-stack robot challenges exist to evaluate robot systems, there remains a notable gap\n\nin long-lasting benchmarks to evalua te a robot system’s progression across multiple levels of robot\n\ncapabilities. Such benchmarks are crucial for providing a comprehensive understanding of the field’s\n\ncurrent standing, now most often around mid-level autonomy (level 3). At the same time, these benchmarks\n\nshould identify gaps that block achievement of higher levels of autonomy. This dual focus gauges how far\n\nrobotics has advanced at a current capability level, while guiding development towards higher-level\n\nintelligence.\n\nTo address these goals, a good benchmark should include the following key elements:\n\n• Levels/stages aligned with multiple levels of robot capabilities: Benchmarks should correspond to\n\nthe different levels of robot capabilities, such as using the scales proposed in this chapter. Fo r\n\ninstance, level 3 could focus on completing a single complex task autonomously, while level 5\n\nwould involve handling multiple tasks and incorporating creative problem solving.\n\n• Tasks should be representative of actual use cases: The tasks in the benchmark should reflect\n\nreal-world use cases to ensure relevance and practicality.\n\n• Participation mechanisms:\n\n˗ Virtual participation: Virtual tracks offer scalability, accessibility and the ability to simulate a\n\nwider range of scenarios, making them a valuable tool for early-stage evaluation and at-scale\n\nevaluations.\n\n˗ Real-world participation: Real-world tracks are critical for testing the full complexity and\n\nunpredictability of physical environments that may be hard to simulate (e.g. network\n\nconnectivity issues). Competitors with high virtual scores could be invited to participate in\n\nreal-world competitions. Competitions, like the DARPA SubT challenge, have both virtual and\n\nreal-world tracks.\n\nTo illustrate this multi-level benchmarking framework, consider an example benchmark for autonomous\n\nurban navigation.\n\n 183\n\nOECD AI CAPABILITY INDICATORS TECHNICAL REPORT © OECD 2025\n\n• Level 1: The robot is tasked with navigating from Point A to Point B along clean, clearly delineated\n\nsidewalks wi